#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/id #uuid "0021ae6a-8ef8-564e-9c2e-fb5f9e8f7313" :question/math-academy-id "e-464" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 2 \\sin {\\left(\\dfrac x 3\\right)} \\,  \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\dfrac x 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac 1 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =3\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/id #uuid "daf5cc74-4c7d-500a-9345-03bd6b73a333" :question/math-academy-id "e-465" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 5 \\csc {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\cot {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\,  \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 6\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =\\dfrac 1 6\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "234c7a86-037c-5316-9419-14db90d1da60" :question/math-academy-id "e-466" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\sec^2\\left(x^3-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u = x^3 - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 3x^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u = x^2\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "03e461a3-0fe0-5fb5-813e-ae23a5dc606a" :question/math-academy-id "e-467" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/4} \\dfrac{\\tan x \\sec^2 x}{1+\\tan^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f75c2c5c-2d41-50a8-a3ce-32c58cb27eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287698} {:answer/id #uuid "97a274ee-df90-514d-983e-ce1e20e74a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287699} {:answer/id #uuid "73adea33-79eb-5a4c-978f-1f0d9d8d0b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287700} {:answer/id #uuid "573baa4f-503b-5dd9-93d2-9c54feaf5a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287701} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8e7c52-5b06-5b28-88b0-e9a26890dc4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73adea33-79eb-5a4c-978f-1f0d9d8d0b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287700} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9a50451-6e29-5ecd-b9bd-77d581f6c3fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a813812e-454b-50d8-be77-8c4858095748" :question/math-academy-id "q-10061" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58c81bd8-a57e-58a7-a1f9-336999dda162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3-9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287705} {:answer/id #uuid "7b29baad-fae9-5bf7-9ecb-c5a11904dc5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3-9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287706} {:answer/id #uuid "baf9df36-02ca-5552-8e3a-3fc3cfdabceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3-9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287707} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc52ccc-210a-5f65-8015-e291b4047f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3-9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287708} {:answer/id #uuid "9823d868-e47d-503d-b39c-6adbc453e2e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3-9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b29baad-fae9-5bf7-9ecb-c5a11904dc5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3-9x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287706} :answer-field/id #uuid "592e2b80-5326-5eec-ad94-43ea28783141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bf0cbc8-cc5a-5684-ae3b-ac70b169300f" :question/math-academy-id "q-10064" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3-9x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=3-9x,$ then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3-9x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3-9x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81755e47-2beb-527e-84ed-50767fe67b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}\\sec (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065369} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d23ab4-1675-55ae-8c22-928190fe5b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065370} {:answer/id #uuid "333eb1d7-82e8-5400-bbce-cbe660277d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sec (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065371} {:answer/id #uuid "57312e2d-e898-5775-8834-f9972fa821e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\sec (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065372} {:answer/id #uuid "5829fcfd-be18-5dde-9581-0270d1968bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sec (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57312e2d-e898-5775-8834-f9972fa821e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\sec (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065372} :answer-field/id #uuid "60902609-13d1-586f-bdab-f6c27d5d80a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ab6145f-e13a-585c-bc5c-d4f390fce17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-10143" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=7x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{7}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(7x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77809e94-9874-5c38-b8c0-34ddd00d75f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡x}{3}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287712} {:answer/id #uuid "31fe31d5-ccda-5b8a-9d49-b9e95a4c5aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287713} {:answer/id #uuid "5997e9f8-5261-55f2-816b-5c2e2dde8c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x}{3}+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287714} {:answer/id #uuid "7aca1100-0ff1-5c3e-989c-0dcc25aca616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡x}{3}+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287715} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb02272-c621-5441-b3d5-152ac85ad705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5997e9f8-5261-55f2-816b-5c2e2dde8c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x}{3}+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287714} :answer-field/id #uuid "f47abc1f-a7bb-583d-bd11-18002f952ccd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "620dd88d-1f8d-5d9a-9cff-83c5c2ba2225" :question/math-academy-id "q-113983" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x+1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x+1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({u}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x}{3}+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e47264a5-3d24-5bbe-b1c4-340691728a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287719} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdee2a0-aa8a-5f9a-a015-05a476fd9c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287720} {:answer/id #uuid "773c3166-c591-50f9-94fd-9d45b6fea28a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287721} {:answer/id #uuid "3ccc9c94-0a0a-5314-a420-a03c44a05fb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287722} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2a8883-3df3-582c-b23a-530b452170b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e47264a5-3d24-5bbe-b1c4-340691728a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287719} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b749213-8398-5e3a-988f-e62395b02832" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "15f12574-67f3-52fd-b2b8-cf7106b117cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-113998" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2}\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f247102-e776-55f3-8c48-50707e3ce329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287726} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec19654-d246-51bb-9115-de759a2c4c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287727} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e3db1d-de75-53bc-a357-8d5e93e9afaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287728} {:answer/id #uuid "1d65046c-a697-5d13-b533-7c7c384f5ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287729} {:answer/id #uuid "996b1fa3-473e-56d9-b56b-b1ca91c58013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡({x}^{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f247102-e776-55f3-8c48-50707e3ce329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287726} :answer-field/id #uuid "d04670c0-aeef-55ff-b651-762f4bef55d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2552dc5-99aa-5d23-af8b-93fb475eed33" :question/math-academy-id "q-13464" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8af228a3-fdfd-53f8-985f-8fcf70a68e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287733} {:answer/id #uuid "47966173-94f2-57cc-a918-8add5b9c0dbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287734} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5d4463-48a3-5405-9e15-6c8ad81ae959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287735} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9251f6-3ba2-566b-bb44-817097d20b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287736} {:answer/id #uuid "ca78ea78-ad41-5163-a53f-c1d86136f972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f9251f6-3ba2-566b-bb44-817097d20b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287736} :answer-field/id #uuid "dba54b9d-c3a7-597f-8162-bfdc70783b7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e33aac6d-c12b-5a93-9e1e-b04561354815" :question/math-academy-id "q-13533" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{\\pi /2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5003d46a-4c07-55ed-81e6-1fe92b31e728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287740} {:answer/id #uuid "22627ab3-b26b-5ab6-9893-12f520e8e17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287741} {:answer/id #uuid "f2cf6ba7-e39c-53ad-9ce8-4e022b373d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287742} {:answer/id #uuid "49906863-b9b5-5885-98a4-f424e1731eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287743} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb54050-161e-54f4-85de-0f3b0e5fedbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49906863-b9b5-5885-98a4-f424e1731eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287743} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b4436a3-16dd-5c6a-8e90-41a53f89f92f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "032f8bbb-4601-5ae2-a6a7-ea4ff94f6117" :question/math-academy-id "q-13537" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{\\pi }$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2\\pi $ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa1e5292-f69a-560b-90f6-dee323d63f42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065627} {:answer/id #uuid "abe4bf0d-7891-5e29-ae2e-55d68fbf39a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065628} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b79a38-7e0f-5eda-9e94-6074e32de133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065629} {:answer/id #uuid "0f3e482d-1dcb-56b4-ae22-7deb8cdb66ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065630} {:answer/id #uuid "644a7d65-fc76-5cf5-88a4-cffec0714227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "644a7d65-fc76-5cf5-88a4-cffec0714227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065631} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a003842-bda8-56d3-879c-1cfcd8f71002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e8b61bc-bb4e-5bb5-ab6c-973e00e2baa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-13647" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc81baf3-eeb4-5370-9bff-9075c8a7c9cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287747} {:answer/id #uuid "679e8ca2-40fa-5082-8beb-c046a0cf351a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287748} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc6d654-89ef-57e5-92ab-b26afdf5de4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287749} {:answer/id #uuid "29701423-b2aa-5699-82fb-4786d80eab5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287750} {:answer/id #uuid "d2548ff1-f212-5f1c-8087-7bfec03ec5cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2548ff1-f212-5f1c-8087-7bfec03ec5cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287751} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc1beb27-57db-57d5-b791-b15ec816b98c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a871d0b5-61bc-5e1e-9eae-078640c38be3" :question/math-academy-id "q-13663" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /6}2{csc}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /9$ | $\\pi /6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /6}2{csc}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "554c1ddc-8f0e-5724-9154-efe619296a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287754} {:answer/id #uuid "19b1582d-6a11-5ebc-9a20-24d157c6c90e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287755} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d9db40-b947-50d4-8c1b-2b8ac7f2553b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287756} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb0223a-b019-51e1-bd32-9824d30787ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287757} {:answer/id #uuid "8719983b-9c53-50ee-aa68-107da10cefa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3d9db40-b947-50d4-8c1b-2b8ac7f2553b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287756} :answer-field/id #uuid "4400dc90-bde0-5101-9561-5c6e59a6889c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ad5d9b8-93ef-5a72-b41c-0d3a93c1add9" :question/math-academy-id "q-2203" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=10x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "926d444a-bcf1-5dea-a87c-e951d9274896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "926d444a-bcf1-5dea-a87c-e951d9274896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287761} :answer-field/id #uuid "7307115c-d6ba-51e9-80ac-f9c77a274d53" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ca6da82-5a4c-5661-86a3-30cf1b78acbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-346081" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{\\pi x}{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8a37403-b885-5788-a56f-6bd95497d5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287764} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d9aafa-3356-542e-84ee-5f0448d1be98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3d9aafa-3356-542e-84ee-5f0448d1be98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287765} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfee3815-d1d6-58ab-8aaa-8e20d47ccf38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db159c18-c8d3-5523-8b58-03253c49fe06" :question/math-academy-id "q-346368" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $2\\pi /3$ | $2\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u))\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =4(-1+2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07bee4df-34be-523b-a39e-11cc153b9414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07bee4df-34be-523b-a39e-11cc153b9414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287768} :answer-field/id #uuid "aba5ed5f-33c8-5bac-80a9-0a1d10b1c047" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51d3460d-2b85-5df6-aaeb-f815320dd08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-350040" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56b4a97d-170f-5c2a-afcd-ba87ae15bf2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287771} {:answer/id #uuid "7f83e2e9-56f5-5e87-bae4-67fa3bcbda49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f83e2e9-56f5-5e87-bae4-67fa3bcbda49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287772} :answer-field/id #uuid "d909989c-8c22-5cd7-bb2e-44221f212a7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46354ead-6195-554d-865f-0c30a629d69a" :question/math-academy-id "q-350202" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /12}}8xsec\\,(4{x}^{2})tan\\,(4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=4{x}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=8x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{\\pi /12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /12}}8xsec\\,(4{x}^{2})tan\\,(4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /12}}sec\\,(4{x}^{2})tan\\,(4{x}^{2})\\cdot 8x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d1eb58-5926-5241-93eb-e8aa11ad7600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d1eb58-5926-5241-93eb-e8aa11ad7600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287775} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb98ddc0-864a-5063-8d20-c9a572909035" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "791c81b3-d8df-54fa-8893-accd7178980e" :question/math-academy-id "q-350203" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi /12}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /6}}12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=6{x}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=12x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=12x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\sqrt{\\pi /12}$ | $\\sqrt{\\pi /6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /2$ | $\\pi $ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi /12}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /6}}12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi /12}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /6}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6{x}^{2})\\cdot 12x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u)\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0-1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0892abf-f18d-50b0-9400-19b799bfd082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(4)-\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0892abf-f18d-50b0-9400-19b799bfd082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(4)-\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287778} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2770ca5-0f64-5e6b-9d33-6c7afcd06ce6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4245d1b-ea43-5712-83b8-544965e9daa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-350887" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)+3}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)+3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)+3}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27306d91-64bd-55c1-ac13-b345eeea365d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(6)-\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287781} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd95e72-5037-5f2b-aef4-5dd99ac7150b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27306d91-64bd-55c1-ac13-b345eeea365d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(6)-\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287781} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d40771b-259f-5872-819b-9d0b3486f7ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31265c46-0cc2-5457-8780-81ed14552e3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-350901" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){cos}^{2}⁡(x)}{8{cos}^{3}⁡(x)+5}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=8{cos}^{3}⁡(x)+5,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{cos}^{3}⁡(x)+5)=-24{cos}^{2}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nSo, differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-24{cos}^{2}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){cos}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $6$ | $5$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){cos}^{2}⁡(x)}{8{cos}^{3}⁡(x)+5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{8{cos}^{3}⁡(x)+5}\\cdot 24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){cos}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{6}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{6}^{5} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95d4c15b-d981-5e38-98d1-c60f45adc960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(4)-\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287785} {:answer/id #uuid "82469416-052c-55b9-9527-ee8b620c1ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95d4c15b-d981-5e38-98d1-c60f45adc960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(4)-\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287785} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac978f2e-4585-5458-89bc-f74c77e4aef4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7392953b-370c-5ac1-9356-c75a7236a22c" :question/math-academy-id "q-350904" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{4}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{4}^{3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4672362-570f-5a2f-904b-10c429d36621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287789} {:answer/id #uuid "c65b881d-b2f3-5877-9c9b-e740b5a15216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287790} {:answer/id #uuid "7339e952-ee52-5f8c-a9c0-b532b11fefbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287791} {:answer/id #uuid "fbeaa44a-1ec4-59f7-a131-8fcf9c803f0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287792} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff91919-aa30-5a15-aaef-a8988ebad7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7339e952-ee52-5f8c-a9c0-b532b11fefbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287791} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcfeac21-43df-5ab3-af65-347ae582c78d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a0d0966-5b44-5ba8-a2dd-7a4e1c591c3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73674" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}).$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})}\\cdot (y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
